9,"d":557,"ds":"Sadama (Estonian for \"Harbour\") is a subdistrict (asum) in the district of Kesklinn (Midtown), Tallinn, the capital of Estonia. It has a population of 2,951 (As of 1 January 2014).","wpu":"Sadama","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/%C3%95huvaade_Sadama_asumile.png/960px-%C3%95huvaade_Sadama_asumile.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Linnahall","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.446667,"lo":24.752778,"d":715,"ds":"Linnahall (Estonian: Tallinna Linnahall, lit. 'Tallinn City Hall'; originally the V. I. Lenin Palace of Culture and Sports) is a semi-abandoned multipurpose venue in Tallinn, Estonia. It is located in the harbor, just beyond the walls of the Old Town, and was completed in 1980. The venue also features a heliport and a small seaport.","wpu":"Linnahall","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/8/87/Linnahall_s%C3%BCgisel.jpg/960px-Linnahall_s%C3%BCgisel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Artius","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.4377,"lo":24.758464,"d":891,"ds":"Artius (formerly Foorum) is a shopping centre in the centre of Tallinn, Estonia. Artius has a gross leasable area of 2,500 square metres containing 28 different shops (including four restaurants and cafés), including women's fashion, footwear, kid's clothes, jewelry, cosmetics and beauty products. The centre has seven floors, with the shops and other commercial services on the ground and first floors.","wpu":"Artius","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/07-06-21-tallinn-by-RalfR-109.jpg/960px-07-06-21-tallinn-by-RalfR-109.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Kohvik Moon","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.445183,"lo":24.747883,"d":967,"ds":"Kohvik Moon is a restaurant in Tallinn, Estonia, located near the Viking Line harbour. The restaurant focuses on slavic food. Despite its small size and non-central location, it is so popular that since its opening in December 2009, the restaurant was almost full of customers every day for several months, and its owners often had to work almost round the clock.","wpu":"Kohvik Moon","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Inside_view_of_Kohvik_Moon%2C_Tallinn%2C_Estonia.jpg/960px-Inside_view_of_Kohvik_Moon%2C_Tallinn%2C_Estonia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"St. Olaf's Church, Tallinn","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.441417,"lo":24.747806,"d":1050,"ds":"St. Olaf's, or St. Olav's Church (Estonian: Oleviste kirik; German: Olaikirche), is a Baptist church in Tallinn, Estonia; originally a Roman Catholic Church, after the Reformation it was a Lutheran church.","wpu":"St. Olaf's Church, Tallinn","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/St_Olaf%27s_church%2C_Tallinn%2C_July_2008.jpg/960px-St_Olaf%27s_church%2C_Tallinn%2C_July_2008.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Great Synagogue of Tallinn","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.434444,"lo":24.757222,"d":1253,"ds":"The Great Synagogue of Tallinn (Estonian: Tallinna suur sünagoog) was an Orthodox Jewish synagogue, that was located in Maakri Street, Tallinn, Estonia. Nowadays, the Jews are using Tallinn Synagogue. The synagogue was built in 1884, and it was designed by Nikolai Thamm Sr.","wpu":"Great Synagogue of Tallinn","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/19/Grande_syna_Tallinn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Gatehouse of the Viru Gate","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.43655,"lo":24.75027,"d":1255,"ds":"The gatehouse of the Viru Gate was a defensive structure built in the 14th century as part of the Tallinn city wall, associated with the Viru Gate. Two watchtowers of this structure remain, which are often mistaken for remnants of the original Viru Gate destroyed in the 19th century during city expansion.","wpu":"Gatehouse of the Viru Gate","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Viru_v%C3%A4ravad_1.jpg/960px-Viru_v%C3%A4ravad_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Church of St. John's almshouse","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.4338,"lo":24.7616,"d":1260,"ds":"Church of St. John's almshouse (Estonian: Tallinna Jaani seegi kirik) is a wooden church in Tallinn, Estonia. Since 1999 the building is designated as \"architectural monument\".","wpu":"Church of St. John's almshouse","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Tallinna_Jaani_Seegi_kirik%2C_14._saj%2C_1648%2C_18._saj_%282%29.jpg/960px-Tallinna_Jaani_Seegi_kirik%2C_14._saj%2C_1648%2C_18._saj_%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Hilton Hotel Tallinn Park","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.433736,"lo":24.768934,"d":1272,"ds":"Hilton Tallinn Park is a hotel within the Kesklinn neighborhood in Tallinn, Estonia. It is on the corner of Kreutzwald, Gonsiori and J. Kunder streets in Tallinn. In front of the hotel, on the other side of the road, is the Police Park (Politseiaed).","wpu":"Hilton Hotel Tallinn Park","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Park_Hotell_-_Hilton_hotell_%2819%29_09.06.16.jpg/960px-Park_Hotell_-_Hilton_hotell_%2819%29_09.06.16.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"St. Catherine's Monastery, Tallinn","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.4375,"lo":24.747778,"d":1281,"ds":"St. Catherine's Dominican Monastery, often simply St. Catherine's Monastery or the Dominican Monastery (Estonian: Dominiiklaste klooster), is a former monastery and one of the oldest buildings in Tallinn, the capital of Estonia.","wpu":"St. Catherine's Monastery, Tallinn","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/P%C3%BCha_Katariina_kirik_2013-06-02.JPG/960px-P%C3%BCha_Katariina_kirik_2013-06-02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Raeapteek","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.437806,"lo":24.746011,"d":1338,"ds":"The Raeapteek (English: Town Hall Pharmacy; German: Ratsapotheke) is a pharmacy in the center of Tallinn, Estonia. Opposite the Tallinn Town Hall, at 11 Raekoja plats, it is one of the oldest continuously running pharmacies in Europe, having always been in business in the same house since the early 15th century. It is also the oldest commercial enterprise and the oldest medical establishment in Tallinn.","wpu":"Raeapteek","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Farmacia_del_Ayuntamiento%2C_Tallin%2C_Estonia%2C_2012-08-05%2C_DD_01.JPG/960px-Farmacia_del_Ayuntamiento%2C_Tallin%2C_Estonia%2C_2012-08-05%2C_DD_01.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Estonian History Museum","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.43843,"lo":24.74484,"d":1353,"ds":"Estonian History Museum (Estonian: Eesti Ajaloomuuseum) is a museum about the history of Estonia in Tallinn. It was initially established by the pharmacist Johann Burchart, who ran the town hall pharmacy known as the Raeapteek. Inaugurated in 1987, it picks up where its counterpart leaves off in the mid-nineteenth century to cover the political and social upheavals of the twentieth century.","wpu":"Estonian History Museum","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Museo_Estonio_de_Historia%2C_Tallin%2C_Estonia%2C_2012-08-05%2C_DD_05.JPG/960px-Museo_Estonio_de_Historia%2C_Tallin%2C_Estonia%2C_2012-08-05%2C_DD_05.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Estonia Theatre","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":59.434722,"lo":24.750833,"d":1395,"ds":"Estonia Theatre is an historic landmark building in central Tallinn, the capital city of Estonia. It houses the Estonian National Opera and the Estonian National Symphony Orchestra.","wpu":"Estonia Theatre","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/a/a8/Tallinn_asv2022-04_img41_National_Opera.jpg/960px-Tallinn_asv2022-04_img41_National_Opera.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Tallinn is the capital and largest city of Estonia. Located on a bay in northern Estonia, on the shore of the Gulf of Finland of the Baltic Sea, it has a population of 452,563 as of 2026, making it the third most-populous city in the Baltics. As of 2024, the population of the Tallinn metropolitan area is estimated at 646,315.","wpu":"Tallinn","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Raekoja_plats_at_night.jpg/960px-Raekoja_plats_at_night.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}